  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways self-propelled Janus particles can be guided by external cues. Across the panels, the particles are shown as two-colored spheres that generate a trailing plume and move directionally in response to their surroundings.\n\nOverall, the image highlights taxis behaviors: movement up a chemical gradient, movement relative to light, combined motion against gravity under illumination, and motion controlled by magnetic fields. In the chemotaxis example, particles migrate toward a region with a higher concentration of “fuel,” indicating positive chemotaxis. The phototaxis schematic shows that the same kind of particle can either move toward the light source or away from it, depending on conditions, labeled as positive and negative phototaxis.\n\nA third panel combines light with gravity: under upward illumination from below, the particle appears to rise opposite the gravitational direction, described as negative photogravitaxis. The final panel illustrates magnetotaxis, where an applied magnetic field aligns the particle orientation and reverses the swimming direction when the magnetic polarity is switched.\n\nTaken together, the figure conveys that active colloidal swimmers can be steered by chemical, optical, gravitational, and magnetic stimuli, enabling controlled motion in different environments.",
